Warren 416: Here's the code, because you can go to the same guy who gave me the data and get the same data, and now you can run that

173
00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:03.290
Warren 416: and build on.

174
00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:07.829
Warren 416: Right? And you can inspect the code, even if you don't have the data.

175
00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:13.120
Warren 416: And say, is what they're doing reasonable, even without looking at the data?

176
00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:21.709
Warren 416: Right? Are you properly handling this aggregation? I don't need the data to sort of say what you're doing there might be wrong.

177
00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:25.819
Warren 416: Right? It might literally be written in code, what you're doing is wrong.

178
00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:36.959
Warren 416: Even before I touched the data. Maybe you're… I know that you didn't read the documentation, and there's missing variables. There's some classical cases where

179
00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:50.559
Warren 416: missing values in years of education was coded as 9-9, but people hadn't removed it because it wasn't this data missing value, right? And so now, all of a sudden, your mean and your median are both completely wrong.

180
00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:54.470
Warren 416: Right, the mean very much so, and the median depends on the distribution, right?

181
00:19:54.930 --> 00:20:03.280
Warren 416: I don't need access to the data to see that that's the case, but that immediately puts anything downstream from that. So, I'm going to assume…

182
00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:12.959
Warren 416: that all of the code that you're doing will get there. At the end, I'll come back to when code itself might be,

183
00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:17.499
Warren 416: Confidential, and again, that's something that you have to build in from the start.

184
00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:27.260
Warren 416: But to sort of go back to some other part you were saying, RCT, If you are collecting data.

185
00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:29.390
Warren 416: You're running a survey.

186
00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.409
Warren 416: If you're running a survey, you've got permission to run the survey.

187
00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:39.039
Warren 416: That permission already locks you in into certain things, and not every…

188
00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:44.390
Warren 416: IRB or Ethics Board may have read,

189
00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:49.600
Warren 416: Michelle Meyer's article that you should be sharing as broadly as possible.

190
00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:56.149
Warren 416: It is actually possible to go back to respondents to ask for permission to share more broadly.

191
00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:59.920
Warren 416: Right? We have done that in some of the surveys that we've run.

192
00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:16.419
Warren 416: You may also have to go back, which we'll get to in a moment, and tell all those respondents that you accidentally leaked their data, which is also not a good outcome, but it definitely proves that you can go back to respondents. I have quite a few number of authors who've had to do that.

193
00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:21.600
Warren 416: through their IRB, go back to their response, say, I accidentally published some of your data.

194
00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:23.709
Warren 416: Which gets me to my next point, Keith.

195
00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.440
