00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:18.769
Warren 416: Right, so you just need to know Can I share the data?

225
00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.240
Warren 416: And you need to keep track of that, and you need to be on the lookout for that.

226
00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:31.649
Warren 416: So one of the ways that if you're collecting your own data.

227
00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:34.980
Warren 416: that you might be constrained is by that IRB approval.

228
00:24:35.330 --> 00:24:36.030
Warren 416: Right.

229
00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:47.399
Warren 416: And whatever you ask your respondents to agree to before answering the survey, there's standard ways of sort of getting consent.

230
00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:49.150
Warren 416: But consent to what?

231
00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:50.300
Warren 416: Right.

232
00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:54.540
Warren 416: So again, formulating those consent agreements.

233
00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:57.469
Warren 416: is something that you negotiate with your IRBs.

234
00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:00.790
Warren 416: That has to be acceptable also to the respondents.

235
00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:02.359
Warren 416: Right? Is it…

236
00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:06.810
Warren 416: If you were to say, I'm going to publish these data broadly, you would probably get less of a response rate.

237
00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:10.500
Warren 416: So there is a balance there as well, but on the other hand.

238
00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:16.300
Warren 416: There are some known techniques to make even your response private.

239
00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:27.949
Warren 416: We'll touch on that a bit later. Randomized response is one of those, right? Randomized response was developed, how can I elicit from you illegal drug use? Well, I'm going to give you

240
00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:34.009
Warren 416: A way to flip a coin, and you're going to tell me something either innocuous or the actual answer.

241
00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:42.510
Warren 416: And I won't know which question you had, I will only know your answer. So I don't know if you answered the question, my birthday is the first half of the year, or…

242
00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:43.839
Warren 416: I use drugs.

243
00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:45.530
Warren 416: Okay.

244
00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:52.129
Warren 416: It's a fancy mathematical method of doing so, except many of the usability studies say most of the respondents don't get the math.

245
00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:57.680
Warren 416: And so that promise that is embedded in math doesn't actually help increase response rates.

246
00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:02.380
Warren 416: They're mostly responding because, I like your face, you're trustworthy, so I'm going to tell you the real answer.

247
00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:04.869
Warren 416: Right? And so,

248
00:26:05.070 --> 00:26:15.989
Warren 416: There are issues with how you formulate those agreements, how much information you have to convey to respondents, and how much they can absorb on these kinds of things as well. That's absolutely not settled.

249
00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:19.360
Warren 416: Right, that's an ongoing discussion as far as I can tell.

250
00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:27.899
Warren 416: But those are baked into the data that you have. Those will determine how broadly or not you can share those data.

251
00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:35.049
Warren 416: Right? Do you… do they have to be on a specific allowed computer? Do they have to be on…

252
00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.139
Warren 416: A university computer.

253
00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:41.139
Warren 416: That broadens it considerably, but also restricts it.

254
00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:46.249
Warren 416: Because it means my collaborator at a different university might not

255
00:26:46.750 --> 00:26:49.360
Warren 416: Be able to access it on their computer.

256
00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:58.510
Warren 416: Maybe I can send them a laptop, a loaner laptop, or something like that, right? So, all of these are going to affect how you process things, and they're baked in from the start.

257
00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:01.300
Warren 416: Probably.

258
00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:05.030
Warren 416: So…

259
00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:14.829
Warren 416: GDPR, here's one thing that has literally reared its head yesterday as well in the US context, because people are making interpretations of that as well.

260
00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:21.979
Warren 416: You might have collected data that is individually public, my CV, my website.

261
00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:23.230
Warren 416: Okay.

262
00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:29.280
Warren 416: Now you're gonna do that for hundreds of researchers. You're gonna scrape the web, all of their public websites.

263
00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:41.610
Warren 416: By GDPR, you've now constructed a database with privacy-enhancing… with privacy-relevant information, and it is now subject to consent. All the data that flowed into it was public.

264
00:27:41.750 --> 00:27:46.990
Warren 416: But by constructing a database, That contained personally identifying information.

265
00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:50.419
Warren 416: You have to not worry about it.

266
00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:52.849
Warren 416: You now have to worry about redistribution.

267
00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:56.830
Warren 416: by GDPR, you cannot.

268
00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:58.919
Warren 416: In part because…

269
00:27:59.230 --> 00:28:05.710
Warren 416: You have collected data and must thus respond to people who want to remove their data from your database.

270
00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:10.530
Warren 416: Once you've disseminated publicly, that's no longer possible, so you become a data controller.

271
00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:15.179
Warren 416: Right? And so now that database, which might be at the core of your research.

272
00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:19.380
Warren 416: Entirely based on public data, but cannot be.

273
00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:37.809
Warren 416: So you have to be aware of these things. This was not clear when GDPR was implemented, and it's sort of moved along. I just yesterday had a case with a U.S. institution, with ICPSR. From the same scenario, the authors had collected public data from various sources.

274
00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:46.489
Warren 416: And wanted to make it available to future researchers, and we said, well, maybe we don't want it to be, like, broadly available, maybe we want just some registration to happen.

275
00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:52.820
Warren 416: Registration is just one level up, but it still would have contained the personally identifying information.

276
00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:56.960
Warren 416: And ICPSR's Disclosure Review Board said.

277
00:28:57.500 --> 00:29:06.930
Warren 416: We don't want to post it at ICPSR, because we would only do that if you strip out all the personal identity information, which makes the data useful.

278
00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:16.450
Warren 416: So… There are considerations about this, that… Emerge from even publicly available.

279
00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:24.759
Warren 416: Historically, also, For instance, if you…

280
00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:34.989
Warren 416: The DHS surveys, for instance, are typically not panel data sets, right? But the PSID, for instance, is a panel data set. It follows people through time.

281
00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:43.719
Warren 416: It has been known for decades that those… being able to follow somebody over time, even if it's just a small sample, is supremely identified.

282
00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:46.929
Warren 416: You can find the people wearing the PSIDE.

283
00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:48.319
Warren 416: Just set your mind to it.

284
00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:55.469
Warren 416: And it has led, for instance, statistical agencies to pull back from publishing panel surveys.

285
00:29:56.520 --> 00:30:02.730
Warren 416: So in Canada, for instance, there's a panel survey, I forget what the acronym stands for, SLIT SLIT.

286
00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:14.349
Warren 416: And they published the first two waves as a panel, and then stopped doing that, because they determined that even though it's a relatively small fraction of the population, by publishing that third year, it was just too identifying.

287
00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:19.140
Warren 416: So, from that moment on, Yes, you can access it directly.

288
00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:20.860
Warren 416: You can access the data.

289
00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:23.180
Warren 416: But only in a restricted environment.

290
00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:24.770
Warren 416: Okay.

291
00:30:26.060 --> 00:30:35.630
Warren 416: Statistics Canada has access to a restricted environment in which they can make the data available, so you literally have to go and travel to a Canadian university, be affiliated with a Canadian university, and walk into a locked room.

292
00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:37.699
Warren 416: To do so.

293
00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:43.730
Warren 416: you probably don't, yourself, have that ability, right? But…

294
00:30:46.160 --> 00:30:54.700
Warren 416: But those abilities are actually present in many, many countries as well, so I did have a… Ugandan?

