539
00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:52.060
Warren 416: Okay? So these K-anonymity things have been out there for a while.

540
00:55:52.620 --> 00:55:56.740
Warren 416: they are… A good compromise between

541
00:55:57.480 --> 00:56:01.570
Warren 416: Modifying the data in a way that you can create a public use data set.

542
00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:06.799
Warren 416: and preserving some anonymity. It still has its weaknesses. There's a litany of what

543
00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:26.149
Warren 416: some in the literature I've called a litany of letters that have come afterwards, L diversity and M-something and T something, that computer scientists have come up with. Once they're on a tangent, they go with that, that have shown weaknesses, so this is not perfect. You can still find situations where you can recover some things.

544
00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:27.210
Warren 416: Right?

545
00:56:28.030 --> 00:56:30.349
Warren 416: Elderstein T-Clustness.

546
00:56:30.610 --> 00:56:33.470
Warren 416: Okay? And…

547
00:56:33.880 --> 00:56:41.750
Warren 416: L diverse and T-Closeness have been found to have weaknesses, so computer scientists love to sort of pick holes into these things and find situations where it fails.

548
00:56:42.020 --> 00:56:44.169
Warren 416: Okay. Is it good enough?

549
00:56:44.670 --> 00:56:47.409
Warren 416: Or not, that's a decision that you have to make.

550
00:56:48.030 --> 00:56:52.519
Warren 416: Okay, so you can play around with this, you can modify to use your own data.

551
00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:58.740
Warren 416: There is also differential privacy.

552
00:56:59.690 --> 00:57:07.679
Warren 416: Which does actually guarantee that you cannot identify people with up to a certain very small probability.

553
00:57:08.030 --> 00:57:11.400
Warren 416: Right? It's a mathematical guarantee if you do it correctly.

554
00:57:12.300 --> 00:57:16.549
Warren 416: I've linked here two articles and a video.

555
00:57:17.070 --> 00:57:18.390
Warren 416: the video…

556
00:57:25.600 --> 00:57:30.109
Warren 416: Essentially demonstrates how differential privacy works, okay?

557
00:57:31.320 --> 00:57:36.749
Warren 416: It works by… Modifying just a tiny bit.

558
00:57:37.800 --> 00:57:40.649
Warren 416: The data, but in a very principled way.

559
00:57:41.410 --> 00:57:42.140
Warren 416: Right?

560
00:57:43.410 --> 00:57:52.350
Warren 416: The downside is that the principled way, Might actually make the data be useless.

561
00:57:53.620 --> 00:57:58.680
Warren 416: So if you actually want to be sure that nobody under no circumstances Ken.

562
00:57:59.150 --> 00:58:01.369
Warren 416: Being re-identified.

563
00:58:03.640 --> 00:58:06.599
Warren 416: Then you might actually have some very coarse data.

564
00:58:09.190 --> 00:58:14.550
Warren 416: That's because every cell gets noise added to it in the simplest implementation.

565
00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:16.950
Warren 416: Okay? And…

566
00:58:18.100 --> 00:58:24.060
Warren 416: Even if there's a ton of people in that cell, you are still going to modify everybody's values, right, by some amount.

567
00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:34.680
Warren 416: So it's a very well-done video for a very complex thing. It's very good, but it sort of illustrates this privacy and accuracy

568
00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:36.230
Warren 416: trade-off.

569
00:58:36.700 --> 00:58:45.250
Warren 416: That all of these methods have, right? You can have perfect accuracy up to whatever errors might be in your survey, which is not trivial to start with.

570
00:58:45.380 --> 00:58:47.739
Warren 416: Right? And the data are not private.

571
00:58:48.250 --> 00:58:56.639
Warren 416: Or you can have perfectly private data, but then it becomes highly inaccurate. And somewhere along that line is something that you want to do.

572
00:58:57.260 --> 00:58:57.990
Warren 416: Okay.

573
00:59:00.290 --> 00:59:01.809
Warren 416: So I'll let you watch that.

574
00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:07.900
Warren 416: We're actually working on an article

575
00:59:08.420 --> 00:59:10.939
Warren 416: That is trying to illustrate that this is not easy.

576
00:59:11.100 --> 00:59:13.090
Warren 416: If you want to do it in a principled way.

577
00:59:15.290 --> 00:59:23.759
Warren 416: We started also from the principle, not that there's a ton of papers that have personal identifying information in there, but that there are a ton of papers that probably haven't done much

578
00:59:24.160 --> 00:59:33.299
Warren 416: in their RCT survey collected data to… they might have de-identified it and moved on, and we know that de-identification is not perfect. So what can we do

579
00:59:33.430 --> 00:59:38.319
Warren 416: To modify the data, and… Maintain its utility.

580
00:59:39.250 --> 00:59:48.930
Warren 416: Right? I want to learn from these RCTs, I want to generalize them and do something. Is the inference that I draw from my RCT going to be affected

581
00:59:49.290 --> 00:59:55.820
Warren 416: By me working with Privatized, or rather, anonymized data.

582
00:59:56.550 --> 00:59:59.890
Warren 416: And what does that imply for transparency?

583
01:00:00.720 --> 01:00:12.680
Warren 416: Right? Because now you need to think, if you're using DHS, you are using post-anonymization data. It has been modified to some extent. You take it as given that the conclusions you come to with a DHS are valid.

584
01:00:15.000 --> 01:00:18.300
Warren 416: That's a data producer, a DHS program doing this.

585
01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:21.229
Warren 416: It may not always be true.

586
01:00:22.140 --> 01:00:25.169
Warren 416: Some of this might be artifacts of the anonymization process.

587
01:00:25.310 --> 01:00:26.020
Warren 416: Right.

588
01:00:26.780 --> 01:00:32.650
Warren 416: So, we go through this example of, here's an RCT with 1,000 people in Sierra Leone.

589
01:00:33.510 --> 01:00:43.610
Warren 416: Can we apply these modern, differentially private, or quasi-differentially private methods, and still come to the same conclusions if we run the analysis on the same thing?

590
01:00:44.670 --> 01:00:51.400
Warren 416: Could we republish this RCT? It's not ours, it's somebody else's. Could we republish this RCT?

591
01:00:51.590 --> 01:00:54.620
Warren 416: With only the… Protect the data.

592
01:00:55.780 --> 01:01:07.340
Warren 416: And then the confidential data stays wherever it was, right? So our conclusions are based on the protected data. Everybody else can also get the protected data and can redo our analysis. Are we going to come to the same conclusion?

593
01:01:13.400 --> 01:01:19.330
Warren 416: So that's… I've identified that there's data out there, I still want to make all the things public.

594
01:01:22.050 --> 01:01:27.420
Warren 416: if I impose on myself the same rules that I would impose on anybody else, so that we see the same thing.

595
01:01:28.040 --> 01:01:31.429
Warren 416: Can we come to the same conclusions? It's not a trivial matter.

596
01:01:33.340 --> 01:01:36.860
Warren 416: Some of the comments we've gotten back from referees are, why don't you just

597
01:01:37.250 --> 01:01:56.580
Warren 416: embed the privacy protection in the survey, right from the start. Going back to the randomized survey, randomized question, randomized response part of it, which, as it turns out, exposed. Differential privacy was sort of developed in the early 2000s. Randomized response goes back to the 60s.

598
01:01:57.130 --> 01:02:04.650
Warren 416: As it turns out, randomized response is differentially private. You can show that it provides a mathematical guarantee that nobody's data can be

599
01:02:04.810 --> 01:02:06.250
Warren 416: Uncovered from that.

600
01:02:07.080 --> 01:02:11.030
Warren 416: And attribute it to that person with a very high public.

601
01:02:12.500 --> 01:02:20.500
Warren 416: Nothing's perfect, so there's always a probability to get hit upon it by accident, but you quantify that probability, and it's really small that you violate somebody's privacy from that.

602
01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:36.440
Warren 416: And the answer to that is, you know, in many cases, that's not possible, right? You're fielding an RCT, you are collecting the data, you want enough precision from that, because if I don't, then there might be many things that I can't do.

603
01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:43.500
Warren 416: There is one strand in the privacy protection literature that quantifies the cost of privacy

604
01:02:43.730 --> 01:02:46.680
Warren 416: As similar to the cost of sample size.

605
01:02:47.310 --> 01:03:02.740
Warren 416: Right? In order to get the same inference precision, you've done your power calculations for some things, in particular for RCTs, right? You can incorporate the uncertainty that you're adding because of the privacy protection into that power calculation. You can quantify it as how many additional

606
01:03:02.800 --> 01:03:10.779
Warren 416: units must I now survey, because I'm going to apply this privacy protection in order to still have power to detect an effect of a certain size?

607
01:03:11.840 --> 01:03:14.840
Warren 416: Increasing sample size means more cost.

608
01:03:15.290 --> 01:03:21.890
Warren 416: Right, so here you are literally quantifying the cost of privacy protection in the survey collection mechanism.

609
01:03:23.180 --> 01:03:27.740
Warren 416: But if that's what you intend to do, if that's what your IRB wants you to do.

610
01:03:28.380 --> 01:03:40.009
Warren 416: then that… now I've just moved the consideration for privacy and the ethical distribution of the data, not even into the conduct of the RCT, not even into the planning of the RCT,

611
01:03:40.080 --> 01:03:52.150
Warren 416: we're pushing it back into the funding of the RCT, right? Because you actually have to request from your funder the funds necessary to incorporate those protection that then your ethics board will sign off on, that you will then collect, that you will then

612
01:03:53.430 --> 01:03:56.949
Warren 416: So you have to be aware of these things way ahead of time.

613
01:03:59.940 --> 01:04:10.829
Warren 416: But there are tools out there to incorporate this into power calculations, right? There are papers that sort of say, okay, you want to apply this method, apply this factor to these things out there.

614
01:04:11.060 --> 01:04:16.440
Warren 416: I'm not an expert on that, and they're not all perfect, but they do exist out there to sort of think about this.

615
01:04:16.660 --> 01:04:19.800
Warren 416: But maybe K-anonymity is sufficient for that.

616
01:04:20.860 --> 01:04:29.510
Warren 416: And… If all of that fails, We can talk about… sharing the…

617
01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:36.079
Warren 416: Data that fails some of the more stringent privacy protections, but has still enough utility to be used.

618
01:04:36.490 --> 01:04:38.479
Warren 416: Through secure distribution mechanisms.

619
01:04:40.420 --> 01:04:44.909
Warren 416: So, before I go to that, any further questions on this?

620
01:04:46.320 --> 01:05:00.390
Warren 416: you've got a bit of homework to go to. I strongly encourage you to have a look at those… the Canonibity Playground and the video, but the article there, the Garfinkel, article, is very well and easy to read,

621
01:05:00.730 --> 01:05:02.370
Warren 416: It's worthwhile to look at it.

622
01:05:03.260 --> 01:05:10.659
Warren 416: It hides a lot of the complexity, but you get what the gist of it is, and what the risks are, which is what the part that you need to worry about.

623
01:05:17.950 --> 01:05:21.149
Warren 416: Part of our back and forth with the referees here is that we have

624
01:05:21.440 --> 01:05:26.689
Warren 416: At some level, referees are saying, yes, but your methods are not perfectly differentially private.

625
01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:39.460
Warren 416: And we're sort of saying yes, but if we do, we can no longer inference what we do here, and we're just doing a post-analysis, so I'm actually thinking that we should actually publish those referee reports as part of the article, but that's a different question.

626
01:05:40.210 --> 01:05:51.359
Warren 416: Okay. I'll just note in passing that one way to partially get around this, but only in combination of what comes afterwards, is synthetic data generation.

627
01:05:52.090 --> 01:05:55.870
Warren 416: So we were talking about data deunification and anonymization.

628
01:05:56.180 --> 01:05:59.980
Warren 416: If you actually say, let me create data that looks

629
01:06:00.220 --> 01:06:03.900
Warren 416: like the data I collected, but is not the data I collected.

630
01:06:05.790 --> 01:06:08.080
Warren 416: So I can still run my analysis through.

631
01:06:08.910 --> 01:06:10.969
Warren 416: Then you're talking about synthetic data.

632
01:06:11.220 --> 01:06:19.550
Warren 416: Again, proponents of synthetic data make it out to be easier than it actually is in practice. I've been involved with synthetic data for

633
01:06:19.710 --> 01:06:21.910
Warren 416: A decade and a half,

634
01:06:23.290 --> 01:06:28.099
Warren 416: You can generate synthetic data pretty easily that makes your code run.

635
01:06:29.670 --> 01:06:32.019
Warren 416: It is an extremely hard endeavor.

636
01:06:32.770 --> 01:06:44.490
Warren 416: To create synthetic data that actually produce results similar to those that you're producing, and it's virtually impossible to do so, to get exactly the result, because now you're back into this differential privacy world.

637
01:06:45.680 --> 01:06:50.929
Warren 416: So, synthetic data generation can help if you can't publish the data because it's too…

638
01:06:51.390 --> 01:06:56.769
Warren 416: Removing or making it anonymous would destroy its utility, then you can create synthetic data.

639
01:06:57.300 --> 01:07:08.160
Warren 416: So instead of Bob, you're gonna call him Jack, and instead of Jill, it's gonna be Jane, or something like that. You can do synthetic data on data, on zip codes, on all sorts of things.

640
01:07:08.950 --> 01:07:12.259
Warren 416: But that your code can still run through, that's perfectly possible.

641
01:07:12.410 --> 01:07:20.370
Warren 416: So that's… that's one way to get transparency, but behind the scenes, you are still generating the results with data that you now no longer publish.

642
01:07:21.140 --> 01:07:23.290
Warren 416: Okay, so what can you do with that?

643
01:07:25.070 --> 01:07:30.470
Warren 416: Well, the honest answer is it's not obvious.

644
01:07:31.800 --> 01:07:33.620
Warren 416: You always can.

645
01:07:34.380 --> 01:07:39.329
Warren 416: Work with your institution to keep the data securely in the institution.

646
01:07:40.060 --> 01:07:40.810
Warren 416: Right?

647
01:07:41.410 --> 01:07:43.410
Warren 416: That is a minimum level.

648
01:07:43.520 --> 01:07:48.800
Warren 416: you still need to think about it. That's not just, oh, I have it in a USB drive in my office.

649
01:07:49.250 --> 01:07:52.059
Warren 416: You probably want to think about, well.

650
01:07:52.220 --> 01:07:56.189
Warren 416: What if I leave my institution, but the data can only stay, say, for now?

651
01:07:56.940 --> 01:08:01.910
Warren 416: I need to hand this off to somebody. And ideally, I don't do this last minute, rather, I plan ahead with that.

652
01:08:02.340 --> 01:08:04.750
Warren 416: It has personally identifying information.

653
01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:21.130
Warren 416: how can it be kept at the university in a way that is worthwhile doing? Something to think about from the start, but it may be part of the discussion all throughout the research about where can I actually run my code on confidential data.

654
01:08:21.680 --> 01:08:22.500
Warren 416: Right?

655
01:08:22.600 --> 01:08:26.630
Warren 416: This is a university computer, it has an encrypted hard drive.

656
01:08:26.770 --> 01:08:34.820
Warren 416: My workstation in my office has an encrypted hard drive, those are minimum requirements that my university imposes on me if I want to keep personalizing information on systems.

657
01:08:36.189 --> 01:08:48.220
Warren 416: But over the years, my university has also told me, and we need, an escrow encryption key for your computer, so that when you are not there to unlock it, the IT staff can unlock it.

658
01:08:51.149 --> 01:08:53.440
Warren 416: Again, what happens when I'm no longer there?

659
01:08:54.399 --> 01:08:59.360
Warren 416: What happens if this falls into the water? Is that the only place that the data ever exists?

660
01:09:00.319 --> 01:09:06.040
Warren 416: So, is it in the backups? Are there things you want to worry about for your research, not even thinking about 5 years ahead?

661
01:09:08.700 --> 01:09:15.520
Warren 416: The downsides with journal policies that require 5 years of retention are that…

662
01:09:16.510 --> 01:09:18.939
Warren 416: You have to answer those emails.

663
01:09:21.840 --> 01:09:30.450
Warren 416: Generically, we as data editors do not like to have to ask the researcher to provide the data post-publication, because

664
01:09:31.220 --> 01:09:38.249
Warren 416: This is a statistic… verified in various publications, about 70% of authors never answered.

665
01:09:38.410 --> 01:09:44.649
Warren 416: When you ask them for data after publication. Publication is done, I don't care about this, I don't want you to have my data, I'm just not answering the email.

666
01:09:46.100 --> 01:09:51.020
Warren 416: So the typical response rate for those kinds of things is on… in the 30%. That's not great.

667
01:09:51.479 --> 01:09:52.550
Warren 416: Right?

668
01:09:53.350 --> 01:10:08.420
Warren 416: But there are also authors who say, sure, I'll answer, but I don't… what if it becomes, like, super popular? I don't want to, like, become DHS program and answer these emails. I don't have… you know, it's me. I don't have staff to handle this, I don't have systems to handle this.

669
01:10:08.630 --> 01:10:13.010
Warren 416: So where can you find systems that you can hand this off to?

670
01:10:16.150 --> 01:10:18.179
Warren 416: Some of it can be institutional.

671
01:10:19.530 --> 01:10:34.900
Warren 416: I don't know about CIDR, but maybe CIDR handles data for his researchers, because they have somebody always on staff whose job it will be to handle this. And it's a small part of that person's job, but now there's mechanisms, there's email inboxes that are monitored and stuff like that.

672
01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:39.839
Warren 416: Right? So going one level up from the researcher to an institute level might work.

673
01:10:39.970 --> 01:10:44.530
Warren 416: I can guarantee you at Cornell, you can't go higher up than that, because everybody will just blow you off.

674
01:10:45.030 --> 01:10:47.360
Warren 416: Nobody wants to do that. Where's the funding?

675
01:10:48.250 --> 01:10:50.090
Warren 416: So that doesn't work.

676
01:10:51.960 --> 01:10:54.799
Warren 416: You can hand it off to data repositories.

677
01:10:55.190 --> 01:11:00.290
Warren 416: Right? So I've got linked to your ICPSR in Dataverse, where you can create

678
01:11:00.750 --> 01:11:05.889
Warren 416: Actually, I should have linked to Zenodo as well, where you can create

679
01:11:06.000 --> 01:11:10.749
Warren 416: repositories, so that they take care of this data it's preserved.

680
01:11:11.320 --> 01:11:18.319
Warren 416: And that are linked to mechanisms where you have to register with an email and agree to some terms of use.

681
01:11:19.050 --> 01:11:21.339
Warren 416: That might be good for some things.

682
01:11:22.040 --> 01:11:35.939
Warren 416: But in other things, when you absolutely cannot de-identify because the names are critical to the research, you're back in the world where you probably are going to require, I needed an IRB to collect these names. You are going to need an IRB approval to use the data that I collected.

683
01:11:36.750 --> 01:11:37.420
Warren 416: Bye.

684
01:11:38.200 --> 01:11:43.230
Warren 416: And neither of these have a simple mechanism to verify that you have IRB.

685
01:11:45.440 --> 01:11:49.190
Warren 416: ICPSR does, but as I just recounted earlier.

686
01:11:49.600 --> 01:11:55.830
Warren 416: they have their own opinion about what data should go there, and that often is much more restrictive than the original IRB actually had.

687
01:11:56.400 --> 01:12:08.280
Warren 416: And it doesn't become very convenient, because they're going to say, we don't believe that you agreed to not put the data someplace, so you're going to run it on our computers in the cloud at ICPSR,

688
01:12:09.290 --> 01:12:13.499
Warren 416: They're not great computers in the cloud at ICPSR, and you have to pay…

689
01:12:14.360 --> 01:12:19.829
Warren 416: $700 for some time period in order to get access to it. I forget if it's per month or per year.

690
01:12:20.730 --> 01:12:22.720
Warren 416: So, they have a mechanism.

691
01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:29.390
Warren 416: You can deposit there, it doesn't cost you any money, any future users would need to accommodate that, right?

692
01:12:31.260 --> 01:12:38.359
Warren 416: But this is indicative of other options that are out there. I mentioned earlier the statistical office in Kapala and Uganda.

693
01:12:38.700 --> 01:12:43.420
Warren 416: Right, so statistical office or national archives sometimes have mechanisms like that.

694
01:12:43.900 --> 01:12:52.290
Warren 416: We have worked in wearing my hat as data with National Archives in Sweden to deposit data that is subject to GDPR.

695
01:12:52.410 --> 01:12:58.790
Warren 416: So somebody needs to verify that the use is compliant with GDPR, and the National Archives handle that.

696
01:12:59.520 --> 01:13:03.190
Warren 416: So there's a Swedish national data archive that does that kind of thing.

697
01:13:03.560 --> 01:13:06.829
Warren 416: Perfect. We plugged it there, off we go.

698
01:13:07.310 --> 01:13:11.920
Warren 416: the French have some sort of national archive, the Germans have some sort of national archive, that…

699
01:13:12.040 --> 01:13:14.889
Warren 416: Has some admissibility criteria that

700
01:13:15.470 --> 01:13:28.110
Warren 416: you may or may not be admissible if you have… if you're working in Germany, and that then have additional things along the background, a mechanism, a workflow, to sort of verify that somebody has IRB approval before accessing.

701
01:13:29.390 --> 01:13:38.680
Warren 416: Right? So this is something to look into, in terms of, is there a capability locally in sort of doing these kinds of things?

702
01:13:41.620 --> 01:13:53.489
Warren 416: There are… I don't know about Latin American archives that do that. I've not seen great stuff there, but Chile has a pretty robust internal architecture for some of these things.

703
01:13:53.590 --> 01:13:58.429
Warren 416: There's actually some decent, institutional architecture in Africa for this.

704
01:13:58.570 --> 01:14:06.029
Warren 416: That has the ability to store, in Africa African data sets with an access protocol around it.

705
01:14:06.270 --> 01:14:10.880
Warren 416: I think they're headquartered in South Africa, but I'm not entirely certain.

706
01:14:11.310 --> 01:14:16.789
Warren 416: So it's worthwhile to look where to offload these data.

707
01:14:17.090 --> 01:14:18.810
Warren 416: Once you're done with your research.

708
01:14:19.290 --> 01:14:21.730
Warren 416: It's also worthwhile to think about, well.

709
01:14:22.620 --> 01:14:31.060
Warren 416: how much after that do we want to keep these data from this one experiment? Should we keep every dataset for 100 years? It's a different question, right?

710
01:14:31.800 --> 01:14:33.590
Warren 416: Journals say 5 years.

711
01:14:34.080 --> 01:14:39.300
Warren 416: Five years is about the decline of citations that happens when stuff goes out there, right?

712
01:14:40.030 --> 01:14:46.719
Warren 416: But I've contacted authors because we've detected, say, on some of the old deposits before I became data editor.

713
01:14:46.840 --> 01:14:53.750
Warren 416: say, 2015, replication package. Turns out somebody used it and found that the data are corrupt.

714
01:14:54.030 --> 01:15:03.799
